CAT = "grolE1Rules"; SEG = "E1R"
Q = [
("A compulsory ship's radiotelephone runs above 1500 W peak envelope power. Who may operate it?",
 "General Radiotelephone licensee", ["Marine Radio Operator Permit holder", "Restricted permit holder", "Any crew member on watch"],
 "Under 47 CFR 80.159, a Marine Radio Operator Permit covers stations up to 1500 W PEP; above that the operator needs a General Radiotelephone Operator License or higher."),
("How long is a General Radiotelephone Operator License valid?",
 "For the holder's lifetime", ["Five years, then renewed", "Ten years, then renewed", "Until a rule change"],
 "The FCC issues the PG for the holder's lifetime, so there is no renewal cycle; replacement is filed in ULS if the document is lost."),
("Who is eligible to hold an FCC commercial radio operator license such as the GROL?",
 "Anyone eligible to work in the US", ["Only US-born citizens", "Only licensed ship masters", "Anyone holding a ham license"],
 "47 CFR 13.9 makes any person legally eligible for employment in the United States eligible, if qualified; citizenship and other licenses are not required."),
("How long must a ship station keep ordinary log entries?",
 "Two years from the entry", ["Six months from the entry", "One year from the entry", "Five years from the entry"],
 "47 CFR 80.409 sets two years from the date of entry, longer for distress logs and logs under investigation or complaint."),
("A station knows of a complaint about one of its logged transmissions. How long must that log be kept?",
 "Until the complaint is settled or barred", ["Two years, then it may go", "Only until the next voyage", "Ninety days from the entry"],
 "47 CFR 80.409 keeps logs tied to a claim or complaint until it is satisfied or barred by the statute of limitations, beyond the usual two years."),
("Who may correct a mistake in a written station log?",
 "Whoever wrote the entry", ["Any operator aboard", "The ship's owner ashore", "An FCC field inspector"],
 "Only the originator may correct an entry, by striking it out, initialing the correction and adding the date; erasing is prohibited."),
("Who must authorize the sending of a distress call from a ship?",
 "Master or person in charge", ["Radio officer acting alone", "Nearest coast station", "Vessel's owner ashore"],
 "47 CFR 80.311 says a distress call and message go out only on the authority of the master or person responsible for the station."),
("Which of these may a ship station use to identify itself during radiotelephone traffic?",
 "Its MMSI", ["Its EPIRB serial", "Its radar band", "Its NAVTEX code"],
 "Part 80 lets ship stations identify by name, call sign or MMSI, and both parties must do so at least once every 15 minutes."),
("What is a ship station forbidden to do under the unauthorized transmissions rule?",
 "Send superfluous traffic", ["Answer a coast station", "Call on a working channel", "Repeat a weather warning"],
 "47 CFR 80.89 bans superfluous radiocommunication, among other things such as selective calling on 2182 kHz or channel 16."),
("A voluntary recreational boat has VHF without DSC. Which channel may replace channel 16 for calling?",
 "Channel 9", ["Channel 13", "Channel 22A", "Channel 70"],
 "47 CFR 80.310 lets noncommercial vessels keep watch on 156.450 MHz (channel 9) instead of channel 16 for call and reply."),
("A ship wants to call a coast station on a working channel. What must its operator do first?",
 "Monitor to check it is clear", ["Send a test signal on it", "Call the FCC for clearance", "Switch to full power"],
 "47 CFR 80.92 requires the operator to determine that the frequency is not in use by monitoring it before transmitting; only distress is excepted."),
("A transmitter technician on duty must have the license available. What satisfies this?",
 "Posting it or a photocopy", ["Knowing the license number", "Leaving it at home safely", "Filing it with the master"],
 "47 CFR 13.19 requires the license, a photocopy, or the application and PPCs to be posted or in the operator's personal possession while on duty."),
]
